From ec1e3f3794fa4084d281b4eeada045c0d15c92c7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 02:42:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] system: a setting to show the internal disks (read-only)

By default the system does not see the disks inside the computer: the entry
that the boot menu starts by itself keeps their drivers from starting (or,
installed on one of those disks, takes the others away and does not mount
the rest of its own) and locks the kernel down. Seeing them meant picking
another entry of the boot menu by hand at every start.

Add a switch for it under System, off by default. The setting is the default
entry of the boot menus and nothing else, so the add-on keeps nothing of it:
it asks /usr/lib/libreelec/internal-disks each time the list is shown and
shows what the menus say, also after a start from the boot menu changed
them. The switch is drawn as on wherever the script can tell that a boot
menu starts the entry that shows the disks, or that nothing hides them: also
when only one of the two menus starts that entry, when a menu that the
script does not know does, and where the entry "run" hides nothing. For
menus that the script does not know and in which nothing hides the disks
(the ones of the LibreELEC installer), and where there is no menu at all, it
is drawn as off.

Turning it on first explains what it means, in a text that is read at one's
own pace: everything that runs here, add-ons from other people included, can
read the disks; read-only is how they are mounted and not a lock; the kernel
runs without its lockdown; it holds on every computer that the drive is
started on; and how to go back. Then it asks, with "No" preselected. Turning
it off asks nothing. Either takes effect at the next start, which the notice
says.

When the boot menus are not the ones of the image, when the system runs in
live mode, or when the change cannot be made, a dialog says why and what
there is to do; when turning it off fails, that the disks may still be shown
at the next start and how to hide them from the boot menu. A system without
the script does not show the setting.

A reset to defaults turns the setting off, once the user has confirmed and
before the reset is asked for: it is not in what a reset clears. Where the
disks would still be shown afterwards, a dialog says so and asks whether to
go on, with "No" preselected.

The script is started with a PATH and a locale of its own, not in the
environment of Kodi, and is never stopped: a change that takes more than a
second is said to be going on, by a bar in a corner of the screen that has
no button.
